WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.775
Standard-library operations participate in failure paths.

2
00:00:02.775 --> 00:00:10.305
Insertion, allocation, copying, moving, hashing, comparison, and element construction can fail depending on the operation and the types involved.

3
00:00:10.305 --> 00:00:14.665
Secure use includes preserving invariants when those operations do not complete.

4
00:00:14.665 --> 00:00:18.233
Prepare work before committing visible state when consistency matters.

5
00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:25.367
Build temporary containers, validate new data, and then swap or move into place through a clear commit point.

6
00:00:25.367 --> 00:00:28.935
This pattern helps preserve existing state if preparation fails.

7
00:00:28.935 --> 00:00:31.313
Shared mutable containers still need synchronization.

8
00:00:31.313 --> 00:00:38.844
The standard library does not turn an unsynchronized shared vector, map, or string into a safe concurrent data structure.

9
00:00:38.844 --> 00:00:44.789
Threads need a clear ownership model, locks, message passing, immutable snapshots, or another documented strategy.

10
00:00:44.789 --> 00:00:47.167
Iterator invalidation and concurrency can interact.

11
00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:54.698
One thread mutating a container while another holds an iterator or view is a design that needs explicit synchronization.

12
00:00:54.698 --> 00:01:00.247
Read-only sharing should still define who owns the lifetime of the underlying data.

13
00:01:00.247 --> 00:01:01.040
Tooling helps.

14
00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:08.175
Compiler warnings, sanitizers, debug iterator modes where available, static analysis, and library diagnostics can expose invalid access, lifetime

15
00:01:08.175 --> 00:01:10.156
mistakes, and boundary issues earlier.

16
00:01:10.156 --> 00:01:13.724
These tools are strongest when paired with targeted tests.

17
00:01:13.724 --> 00:01:20.858
Tests should cover boundary sizes, empty containers, invalid indexes, mutation during iteration, container growth, erased elements, moved objects,

18
00:01:20.858 --> 00:01:22.047
and failure paths.

19
00:01:22.047 --> 00:01:27.200
Regression tests should be added when a lifetime or invalidation defect is fixed.

20
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.164
Code review should treat standard-library assumptions as security-sensitive.

21
00:01:31.164 --> 00:01:37.505
Ask whether any iterator, reference, pointer, view, index, or size assumption can become invalid before use.

22
00:01:37.505 --> 00:01:40.676
Also ask whether input can drive unbounded growth.

23
00:01:40.676 --> 00:01:48.604
Review workflow should include the specific standard-library operation, not just the container type. push_back, erase, reserve, sort, insert, and

24
00:01:48.604 --> 00:01:51.775
rehash each have different lifetime and failure implications.

25
00:01:51.775 --> 00:01:54.549
Naming those operations makes review more precise.

26
00:01:54.549 --> 00:01:58.513
Concurrency review should also cover ownership of views and iterators.

27
00:01:58.513 --> 00:02:09.611
A lock that protects the container during lookup is not enough if a caller stores a view and uses it after the lock is released and another thread

28
00:02:09.611 --> 00:02:10.800
mutates the source.
